      "text/plain": [
       "{'problem_id': 'Goedel-Pset-133',\n",
       " 'formal_statement': 'import Mathlib\\nimport Aesop\\n\\nset_option maxHeartbeats 0\\n\\nopen BigOperators Real Nat Topology Rat\\n\\n/-- The number of permutations of 8 fruits with 4 identical apples, \\n    2 identical oranges, 1 unique banana, and 2 identical pears is 420 -/\\ntheorem fruit_permutations : \\n  Nat.factorial 8 / (Nat.factorial 4 * Nat.factorial 2 * Nat.factorial 1 * Nat.factorial 2) = 420 := by sorry',\n",
       " 'informal_statement': 'Cory has $4$ apples, $2$ oranges, $1$ banana, and $2$ pears. He decides to eat one piece of fruit per day for $8$ days and the pieces of fruit within each category are indistinguishable. In how many different sequences can Cory eat his fruits over the 8 days?',\n",
       " 'informal_solution': 'Cory has a total of $9$ fruits, consisting of $4$ apples, $2$ oranges, $1$ banana, and $2$ pears. He eats over $8$ days, thus he will eat all the fruits in the 8 days.\\nThe number of distinct sequences in which Cory can eat these fruits can be calculated using the formula for permutations of a multiset:\\n\\n$$ \\\\frac{n!}{n_1! \\\\times n_2! \\\\times n_3! \\\\times n_4!} $$\\n\\nwhere \\\\( n \\\\) is the total number of fruits, \\\\( n_1 \\\\) is the number of apples, \\\\( n_2 \\\\) is the number of oranges, \\\\( n_3 \\\\) is the number of bananas, and \\\\( n_4 \\\\) is the number of pears.\\n\\nPlugging in the numbers:\\n$$ \\\\frac{8!}{4! \\\\times 2! \\\\times 1! \\\\times 2!} = \\\\frac{40320}{24 \\\\times 2 \\\\times 1 \\\\times 2} = \\\\frac{40320}{96} = \\\\boxed{420} $$\\n\\nConclusion:\\nThe number of distinct sequences in which Cory can eat the fruits over 8 days is $\\\\boxed{420}$.',\n",
       " 'is_proof_problem': False}"
      ]
